{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve for the smallest positive integer \\( t \\) such that \\(\\mathop{\\text{lcm}}[12,t]^3 = (12t)^2\\), we start by expressing 12 and \\( t \\) in terms of their prime factorizations. Let \\( 12 = 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\) and \\( t = 2^a \\cdot 3^b \\cdot k \\), where \\( k \\) is an integer not divisible by 2 or 3. The least common multiple of 12 and \\( t \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathop{\\text{lcm}}[12, t] = 2^{\\max(2, a)} \\cdot 3^{\\max(1, b)} \\cdot k\n\\]\n\nWe need to cube this least common multiple and set it equal to \\((12t)^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathop{\\text{lcm}}[12, t]^3 = (2^{\\max(2, a)} \\cdot 3^{\\max(1, b)} \\cdot k)^3 = 2^{3\\max(2, a)} \\cdot 3^{3\\max(1, b)} \\cdot k^3\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(12t)^2 = (2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2^a \\cdot 3^b \\cdot k)^2 = 2^{2(2+a)} \\cdot 3^{2(1+b)} \\cdot k^2\n\\]\n\nEquating the two expressions, we get:\n\n\\[\n2^{3\\max(2, a)} \\cdot 3^{3\\max(1, b)} \\cdot k^3 = 2^{2(2+a)} \\cdot 3^{2(1+b)} \\cdot k^2\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n3\\max(2, a) = 2(2+a) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3\\max(1, b) = 2(1+b) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad k^3 = k^2\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1005,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,369,279,24632,6785,7546,17767,259,1124,8,1741,429,1124,11520,10374,453,35702,1318,94617,6226,3417,58,16,17,13960,90304,18,284,320,16,17,83,29776,17,59,701,582,1191,553,36710,220,16,17,323,17767,259,1124,8,304,3793,315,862,10250,8168,8040,13,6771,17767,220,16,17,284,220,17,61,17,1124,50853,220,18,1124,8,323,17767,259,284,220,17,61,64,1124,50853,220,18,61,65,1124,50853,595,1124,701,1380,17767,595,1124,8,374,458,7546,537,74916,553,220,17,476,220,18,13,576,3245,4185,5248,315,220,16,17,323,17767,259,1124,8,374,2661,553,1447,59,9640,59,10374,453,35702,1318,94617,6226,3417,58,16,17,11,259,60,284,220,17,61,35702,2810,7,17,11,264,9139,1124,50853,220,18,61,35702,2810,7,16,11,293